'''''Sphagnum tenellum''''' is a species of [[moss]] belonging to the family [[Sphagnaceae]].<ref name="GBIF">{{cite web |title=Sphagnum tenellum Bridel, 1819 |url=https://www.gbif.org/species/2669251 |website=www.gbif.org |access-date=19 February 2021 |language=en}}</ref>

It is native to Europe, Japan and America<ref name="GBIF" /> and is the smallest British ''[[Sphagnum]]''.<ref name="BBS-tenellum">{{cite web | title = ''Sphagnum tenellum'' | url = https://www.britishbryologicalsociety.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Sphagnum-tenellum.pdf | website = British Bryological Society | access-date = 19 April 2026}}</ref>

==Characteristics==
This moss type occurs predominantly as small patches or cushions, between 10-20 cm across. It can also appear as scattered stems. It is usually a yellow-orange or yellow-brown colour, but can tend to appear as a more dull green colour when submerged. The branch leaves are noted as strongly concave in shape.<ref name="BBS-tenellum" />

==Habitat==
''Sphagnum tenellum'' has been noted to appear on acidic, bare and peaty ground, more specifically in bogs, wet heaths and humid, heather-based slopes in areas of high rainfall.<ref name="BBS-tenellum" />

